## Intended Page Join owner interface

Current Page Join is memory-only. `page/data.ts:168–230` parses/adopts/signs/submits one Join without durable exact-envelope custody. `page/main.ts:49–86` owns settings/readback/key selection; `99–103` keeps memory Join maps; `255–365` owns explicit Join UI. No provisional fragment capture or replaceState exists. `page/claim.ts:72–132` explicitly rejects Config.join and invitation private records. Claim and U1 voice journals are different domains and must not be borrowed as an enrollment store. Existing I2 owns the single future Page enrollment journal; the interface below is intended, not delivered.

```ts
interface EnrollmentCustody {
  capture(invitation: ParsedInvitation, trust: InvitationTrust): Promise<ProvisionalId>;
  status(id: ProvisionalId): Promise<PublicEnrollmentStatus>;
  join(id: ProvisionalId, options: { current(): boolean }): Promise<EnrollmentOutcome>;
  reconcile(id: ProvisionalId, options: { current(): boolean }): Promise<EnrollmentOutcome>;
  resumePrepared(id: ProvisionalId, options: { current(): boolean }): Promise<EnrollmentOutcome>;
}
```

Capture and status generate no signer, signature, POST or activation. Join is explicit enrollment. Reconcile reads the same uncertain original; Resume, if selected by the owner, first-dispatches only a definitely-unsent prepared original. Public status exposes phases, operation ID, public key/full refs and safe reasons; raw fragments, secrets, signed envelopes and private keys never enter UI diagnostics, logs, workroom messages, screenshots or exports.

Use one owner-selected IndexedDB journal with atomic metadata/record transactions and the existing Page identity manager. Reconcile key persistence with current localStorage before implementation: no silent key replacement or competing signer. Proposed global metadata Web Lock `artroom-page-enrollment:v1` serializes quota and operation creation; operation locks plus database generation/CAS exclude simultaneous tabs. When both locks are required, acquire global then operation, and release global before network waits. Commit completion and bounded readback under operation lock precede sending.

Required bounds are 32 records, 4MiB aggregate and 64KiB canonical UTF-8 per record. The record caps already imply at most 2MiB of record bytes; 4MiB remains an additional aggregate ceiling, not extra record capacity. Key/metadata/proof byte accounting must be explicitly selected. Unknown/malformed records remain intact; no silent eviction. Terminal archives count unless the owner adopts a separate reviewed policy.

## Custody sequencing

1. Bound, parse and independently bind before any side effect. Under metadata lock, retain the original bounded invitation bytes, codec version, compatibility fingerprint and hint bindings in a provisional record. Commit and read back. **Only then** strip the fragment with same-origin fixed-path `history.replaceState`. A lock/write/readback failure leaves the fragment available locally, generates no key and sends no Join. Bootstrap/assets requests contain no fragment/referrer; native reads/navigation wait for capture.
2. Explicit Join acquires the operation lock and checks current identity/generation. Select the existing local key or generate a key only now. Retain key-bound unsigned preparation and operation identity before awaited signing. Build the actual native Join from the exact supported declaration; no unauthorized pre-Join membership read is assumed.
3. Commit the exact closed signed envelope, request digest, link fingerprint and bindings. Read back and verify signature/full target/key/fields. Commit an inflight ticket and read it back **before POST**. A crash after inflight is unknown even if sending never began. Failed persistence or unknown delivery never rekeys, resigns or blind-resubmits. A crash during unsigned preparation can finish only that same preparation and key. A definitely-unsent prepared original needs a separate explicit Resume choice.
4. Retain a matching accepted receipt before awaited reads. Verify full fact, pin, intent, recomputed entry hash and exact original signed bytes, admitted member/key and inbox creation. Contradictory receipt blocks readiness. Unconfirmed acceptance remains original read-only settlement, not a completed Join. A changed head or another link cannot settle it.
5. Obtain a real supported session bound to trusted deployment. Verify native directory/application creation source/result/full refs/incarnation/digest and current standing/actions. Only then commit a verified public app binding privately with its own key, and change selected Page settings under generation/readback. No hand-copied CLI key file or binding JSON is required. Participate creates an inactive row; voice readiness and explicit Activate are later distinct actions.

CLI keeps its actual PendingJoin/Store owner. `cli/commands.ts:710–786` saves private signed bytes and a public pointer before POST, preserves the decoded v1 canonical fingerprint, verifies original entry and recovers the same key. `cli/files.ts:35–54,66–96` provides atomic/exclusive owner-only files but **no cross-process metadata lock/CAS**. Add/reconcile its actual lock/readback under that owner; do not claim the Page Web Lock already solves CLI continuity.

## Tabs, reload and new devices

Tabs sharing the origin use one operation/key/envelope generation. Another redemption waits on the lock and observes saved prepared/inflight/unknown state; it cannot create a second signer or replace uncertain custody with another link. Reload recovers the same operation/key/preparation/envelope/proof without automatic Join, activation or sound. Settings changes detach presentation but retain original private work. Callbacks check captured identity/generation before painting or selecting config. Missing key/storage is blocked recovery, not a new identity fallback.

A new device has no old key/journal and cannot recover that enrollment by assertion. It needs a separate native key invitation for the same member or a named new-member invitation, with explicit permissions and capacity. Native invite-member creates a person; `--role agent` changes role rather than agent kind/controller. Actual agent enrollment uses add-member with agent/controller then invite-key/enrol. Invitation metadata changes none of these rules, session windows or M1 live member/key limits. Independent key-loss/device recovery remains parent work.

